UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 23, Lesson 1: Who makes rules', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Different people make rules in different places. Carers make rules at home, and the principal and teachers make rules at school. Local councils make local laws, state governments make state laws and the federal government makes laws for the whole country. Police enforce laws and courts decide when a law has been broken.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhich level of government is most likely to make each: parking rules in the town centre; school starting age in the state; the rules for passports?\n\nFEEDBACK\nLocal council: parking in the town centre; state government: school starting age; federal government: passports.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFind one rule or service from each level of government using a reliable source such as a council website. Record who makes it and why. Draw a diagram showing who makes rules at home, at school and in government.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Different people make rules in different places. Carers make rules at home, and the principal and teachers make rules at school. Local councils make local laws, state governments make state laws and the federal government makes laws for the whole country. Police enforce laws and courts decide when a law has been broken.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-GEO-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Find one rule or service from each level of government using a reliable source such as a council website. Record who makes it and why. Draw a diagram showing who makes rules at home, at school and in government. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using council on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles.. Correction: Local council: parking in the town centre;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating council on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with council, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about council with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for council. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for council. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Local council: parking in the town centre","Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Which level of government is most likely to make each: parking rules in the town centre; school starting age in the state; the rules for passports?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for council; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of state and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles","Different people make rules in different places","Local council: parking in the town centre"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with council?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named state and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles","Name state and explain why it matters only, without linking it to council","Apply council to new material and name the state and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies council with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence appropriately for this lesson on council. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles","Local council: parking in the town centre","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail"],"question":"Which response best uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence for council?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles","Name a specific source or place detail and say what it shows about council"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about council?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Different people make rules in different places.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: dogs must be on a lead in a park is a local council law","Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about council?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check facts, acknowledge limits of the evidence, and avoid inventing details","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps sources, places or historical/geographic evidence accurate for council?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles","Name state and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Find one rule or service from each level of government using a reliable source such as a council website","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies council to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about council with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating council on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using council on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for council. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with council, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles.. Correction: Local council: parking in the town centre;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w23-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise council.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Civics and Citizenship","success_criteria":["I can use council with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify state and explain why it matters.","I can practise federal independently on a new prompt.","I can notice government needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Different people make rules in different places.","options":["Different people make rules in different places","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about council with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: dogs must be on a lead in a park is a local council law. Speed limits on roads are set by the state government. Passports are a federal matter. A court hears a case when someone is said to have broken the law.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhich level of government is most likely to make each: parking rules in the town centre; school starting age in the state; the rules for passports?\n\nFEEDBACK\nLocal council: parking in the town centre; state government: school starting age; federal government: passports.","explain":"Different people make rules in different places. Carers make rules at home, and the principal and teachers make rules at school. Local councils make local laws, state governments make state laws and the federal government makes laws for the whole country. Police enforce laws and courts decide when a law has been broken.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Levels of government and laws.","caption":"Match the rule to the level.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Local council</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">dogs on a lead</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">State</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">speed limits</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: dogs must be on a lead in a park is a local council law. Speed limits on roads are set by the state government. Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but 