th the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Federal laws and the role of courts.","caption":"Making, enforcing and deciding are different jobs.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Federal</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">passports</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Courts</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">decide if a law is broken</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about council with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles. Here is a more productive model: Example: dogs must be on a lead in a park is a local council law. Speed limits on roads are set by the state government. Passports are a federal matter. A court hears a case when someone is said to have broken the law.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nLocal council: parking in the town centre; state government: school starting age; federal government: passports.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Believing one person or group makes all rules ignores that home, school and three levels of government each have roles. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about council with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDifferent people make rules in different places. Carers make rules at home, and the principal and teachers make rules at school. Local councils make local laws, state governments make state laws and the federal government makes laws for the whole country. Police enforce laws and courts decide when a law has been broken. Now